  "why_this_direction": "The first direct-gradient attempt reproduced the complete original native measurement exactly, then exited before differentiation because GPU allocator peak statistics were reset before this controller initialized CUDA. Initialize CUDA before that observational reset, retain the same computational update and data, and run the original-to-fresh-path experiment under a new immutable declaration.",
  "repeating_pattern_check": "Preserve the failed attempt and its exact baseline; do not misreport a setup failure as a computational result or change the numerical rule. A real cold-CUDA context test checks initialization/reset before this relaunch. No inverse/search, source mutation of a running controller, token scoring or tolerance retuning.",
  "hypothesis": "Repeated full computational-loss weight derivatives, each scaled by the current joint directional response and followed by native BF16 installation and fresh generation, can close this one wrong computation against the unchanged original correct computation.",
  "what_went_wrong_before": "At03:59:47CT the first direct attempt exited at reset_peak_memory_stats with Invalid device argument; native generation ran in subprocesses, leaving the controller allocator uninitialized. Its fresh baseline was0.00029234223350039747 with4386outside; no gradient or weight update occurred.",
  "rule": "For the complete fixed original radial residual r and current complete held correspondence: differentiate L=sum(r^2) once through the native-anchored graph into all 70 matrices; S=-dL/dW; measure q=J_r S once; alpha=-dot(r,q)/dot(q,q). Round W+alpha*S once to native BF16, publish all matrices, generate only the same training member from the reached file, and rebuild complete computational correspondence/measurement. No CGLS, candidate search, rate schedule, output scoring or position cut. Gradient/response/rounding stalls with outside cells are nonclosure. Only complete fresh native zero outside after an installed update is closure.",
  "prediction": "The first correction yields one fully written BF16 update and a subsequent fresh native computational measurement. Across the refreshed loop the fixed computational discrepancy decreases and ultimately closes; each actual native observation is reported even if it rises. No linear prediction or written state alone is success.",
  "would_weaken_it": "Zero derivative/response or no representable BF16 change with native divergence, repeated fresh-path loss growth or failure to close, or incomplete acquisition weakens this construction; none is hidden by a new tolerance, reference, inner search or answer judgment. Single-pair closure alone does not prove transfer.",
